-- title:
--   Theorem 5.4 (companion theorem) — singular-control MFG with geometric dynamics and common noise: the solutions form a nonempty complete lattice reached by best-reply iteration
-- statement:
--   Consider the mean field game of §5.1: two independent Brownian motions $W,B$ on a complete filtered probability space, the common-noise filtration $\mathbb F^o$ generated by $\sigma^oB$, monotone controls $\xi\in V^\uparrow$, the geometric state
--   $$X^\xi_t=\mathcal E_t\Big[x_0+\int_{[0,t]}\mathcal E_s^{-1}\,d\xi_s\Big],\qquad \mathcal E_t=\exp\Big[\Big(b-\frac{\sigma^2+(\sigma^o)^2}{2}\Big)t+\sigma W_t+\sigma^oB_t\Big],$$
--   and the cost $J(\xi,\mu)=E\big[\int_0^Tf(t,X^\xi_t,\mu_t)\,dt+g(X^\xi_T,\mu_T)+\int_{[0,T]}c_t\,d\xi_t\big]$, under Assumption 5.1 with $p=2$ and Assumption 5.3. An equilibrium is an $\mathbb F^o$-progressive flow $\mu$ of probability measures with an optimal control $\xi^\mu$ such that $\mu_t=P[X^{\xi^\mu}_t\in\cdot\mid\mathcal F^o_T]$ a.s. for every $t$ (Definition 5.2). $L$ is the lattice of such flows between $\mu^{Min}$ and $\mu^{Max}$ of (5.13)–(5.14), with $\pi=dt+\delta_T$, and $R(\mu)_t=P[X^\mu_t\in\cdot\mid\mathcal F^o_T]$.
--
--   1. The set of MFG solutions is nonempty, and every nonempty subset of it has a least upper bound and a greatest lower bound in it.
--   2. If $f$ and $g$ are continuous in $(x,\mu)$, then the sequence $\mu^0=\inf L$, $\mu^{n+1}=R(\mu^n)$ is nondecreasing in $L$, a least solution $\underline\mu$ exists, and $\mu^n_t\to\underline\mu_t$ weakly, $P$-a.s., for $\pi$-a.e. $t$.
--   3. Under the same condition, $\mu^0=\sup L$, $\mu^{n+1}=R(\mu^n)$ is nonincreasing, a greatest solution $\bar\mu$ exists, and $\mu^n_t\to\bar\mu_t$ weakly, $P$-a.s., for $\pi$-a.e. $t$.
--
--   This is the first application of the abstract Theorem 2.6 to singular control with common noise.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $L$ is built from any random field $S$ with the defining properties of the essential supremum $\operatorname{ess\,sup}_\mu E[|X^\mu_t|^2\mid\mathcal F^o_T]$; elements of $L$ are processes, solutions and best replies are taken up to $\le_L$-equivalence, and $\mu^{n+1}=R(\mu^n)$ means $\mu^{n+1}\in R(\mu^n)$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: the learning procedure … converges to the minimum (maximal) MFG solution → a least (greatest) element of M exists and µⁿ converges weakly to each of its representatives, a.e. as in the paper's proofs; 'maximal' read as greatest; reason: the order limit equals the weak limit by Lemma 3.1; the proofs (Theorem 2.6, (7.10)) establish exactly this
--   - reading: the set of solutions … is a nonempty complete lattice; µⁿ⁺¹ = R(µⁿ) → solutions are the elements of L equivalent to a Definition 5.2 equilibrium; complete lattice in the order induced from L (IsLUB/IsGLB of every nonempty subset within the solution set); µⁿ⁺¹ ∈ R(µⁿ); reason: L is a set of classes and R is single-valued up to ≤_L-equivalence (unique optimal pair, p.16)
--   - encoding: the strong solution of (5.2) Xξ → the explicit form (5.4) as the definition of Xξ; reason: the paper's own P-a.s. identity (p.17); no stochastic integral is needed
--   - encoding: µ^Min, µ^Max from ess sup_µ E[|X^µ_t|² | F°_T] (5.13)–(5.14); complete filtered probability space → L defined from every S characterized as that essential supremum, via distribution functions with x/0 = ∞; F₀ contains the P-null sets; reason: the essential supremum is unique a.s.; the inequalities between laws are those of (5.13)–(5.15)
--   - encoding: J(ξ, µ) = E[…]; conditional laws P[X_t ∈ · | F°_T] P-a.s. → J in [−∞, ∞] (E[Y⁺] − E[Y⁻]); identities for each Borel set a.s.; reason: the cost may be +∞; a measure-valued version satisfying the identities for every Borel set is a regular conditional law
--   - encoding: weak convergence → WeakConv (survival functions at continuity points); reason: for probability measures bounded below in ≤st by a probability measure and a probability limit, it coincides with narrow convergence
--   - encoding: f and g continuous in (x, µ) → ContInXMu: sequential continuity for the weak convergence; reason: the weak topology is metrizable (p.9)
-- source:
--   Dianetti, Ferrari, Fischer, Nendel, A Unifying Framework for Submodular Mean Field Games, arXiv:2201.07850v1, §5.1.6, Theorem 5.4, p.19 (model §5.1.1–5.1.5, pp.16–19)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SubmodMFG_Supplement_SubProb
import Definitions.Def_SubmodMFG_Supplement_Processes
import Definitions.Def_SubmodMFG_Supplement_SingularCommon
import Definitions.Def_SubmodMFG_Supplement_GBMCommonNoise

open MeasureTheory

namespace SubmodMFG.Supplement

/-- **Theorem 5.4** — the singular-control MFG with geometric dynamics and common noise: the
solutions form a nonempty complete lattice, and best-reply iteration from `inf L` / `sup L`
converges monotonically to the least / greatest solution.
Dianetti, Ferrari, Fischer, Nendel, *A Unifying Framework for Submodular Mean Field Games*,
arXiv:2201.07850v1, §5.1.6, Theorem 5.4, p.19: "The set of solutions of the MFG with singular
controls and common noise is a nonempty complete lattice. Moreover, if f and g are continuous in
(x, µ), then (1) the learning procedure µⁿ defined inductively by µ⁰ = inf L and µⁿ⁺¹ = R(µⁿ) is
nondecreasing in L and it converges to the minimum MFG solution, (2) the learning procedure µⁿ
defined inductively by µ⁰ = sup L and µⁿ⁺¹ = R(µⁿ) is nonincreasing in L and it converges to the
maximal MFG solution."

**Formalization Note.** Standing assumptions of §5.1 (Assumptions 5.1 with `p = 2` and 5.3);
`L` is built from the essential supremum `S` of (5.13)–(5.14) (every `S` with the defining
property). "Complete lattice" is in the order induced from `L`: every nonempty subset of the
solution set has a least upper and a greatest lower bound in it. `µⁿ⁺¹ = R(µⁿ)` is
`µⁿ⁺¹ ∈ R(µⁿ)` (`R` is single-valued up to `≤_L`-equivalence); `µ⁰` is a bottom (top) of `L`.
"Converges to the minimum (maximal) MFG solution": a least (greatest) solution exists and, for
each of its representatives `e`, `µⁿ_t → e_t` weakly P-a.s. for `π`-a.e. `t`; "maximal" is read
as greatest. "Continuous in (x, µ)" is `ContInXMu`. -/
theorem theorem_5_4 {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (D : GBMModel Ω) (hD : D.Standing)
    (S : ℝ → Ω → ℝ) (hS : D.IsEssSupMoment S) :
    ((D.equilibria S).Nonempty ∧
      (∀ F : Set (D.equilibria S), F.Nonempty → ∃ b, IsLUB F b) ∧
      (∀ F : Set (D.equilibria S), F.Nonempty → ∃ b, IsGLB F b)) ∧
    (ContInXMu D.T D.f D.g →
      (∀ m : ℕ → D.FlowL S, IsBot (m 0) → (∀ n, m (n + 1) ∈ D.R S (m n)) →
        Monotone m ∧ (∃ e, IsLeast (D.equilibria S) e) ∧
        ∀ e, IsLeast (D.equilibria S) e →
          ∀ᵐ t ∂D.π, ∀ᵐ ω ∂D.P, WeakConv (fun n => (m n).1 t ω) (e.1 t ω)) ∧
      (∀ m : ℕ → D.FlowL S, IsTop (m 0) → (∀ n, m (n + 1) ∈ D.R S (m n)) →
        Antitone m ∧ (∃ e, IsGreatest (D.equilibria S) e) ∧
        ∀ e, IsGreatest (D.equilibria S) e →
          ∀ᵐ t ∂D.π, ∀ᵐ ω ∂D.P, WeakConv (fun n => (m n).1 t ω) (e.1 t ω))) := by sorry

end SubmodMFG.Supplement
